WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 41: The Schoolchildren's Blizzard of 1888: Terror on the Dakota Prairie

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:16.880
[Rebecca] In January 1888, the vast, treeless prairie of the Dakota Territory was home to tens of thousands of immigrant homesteaders who had arrived to claim free land under the Homestead Act.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:24.320
[Rebecca] Life in sodhouses was hard, but on the morning of Thursday, January 12th, winter seemed to relax its icy grip.

5
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.240
[Rebecca] The morning broke unnaturally mild and balmy, with temperatures rising into the upper 30s.

6
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:39.840
[Rebecca] Believing that a gentle spring thaw had arrived, parents sent their children to distant one-room wooden schoolhouses wearing light sweaters, leaving their heavy coats and mittens at home.

7
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.400
[Rebecca] But high in the Canadian Arctic, a colossal wall of Siberian air was plunging southward at 60 miles an hour.

8
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:56.960
[Rebecca] At midday, the blue sky turned ink black, and in less than three minutes, the prairie was engulfed in the deadliest, most sudden blizzard in American history, the schoolchildren's blizzard.

9
00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:01.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:13.360
[George] The winter of 1887 and 1888 had already been brutal across the northern plains, burying the sod houses and claimed shanties of Dakota Territory under towering drifts of snow.

11
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:20.000
[George] But on the morning of Thursday, January 12th, an extraordinary meteorological trap was sprung.

12
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:30.800
[George] A high-altitude warm air mass pushed northward from the Gulf of Mexico, causing temperatures to skyrocket from near zero at dawn to almost 40 degrees above zero by mid-morning.

13
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:40.080
[George] Melting snow dripped pleasantly from wooden eaves, water ran in sparkling rivulets down prairie ruts, and balmy breezes felt like early spring.

14
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:47.520
[George] Homesteaders stepped outside in their shirt sleeves to chop firewood, air out winter bedding, and turn cattle out to pasture.

15
00:01:47.520 --> 00:02:02.520
[George] Across the territory, thousands of young boys and girls, the children of German, Norwegian, Swedish, and Bohemian immigrant homesteaders, set out on foot across the flat, featureless grassland to their local one-room country schoolhouses.

16
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:10.600
[George] Many walked three or four miles across open country without a single tree, fence line, or landmark to guide them.

17
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:23.720
[George] Relieved by the pleasant air, children begged their mothers to leave their heavy buffalo coats, wool scarves and oversized mittens at home, setting off across the melting prairie in light woolen sweaters and cloth caps.

18
00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:35.960
[George] By nine o'clock in the morning, the small wooden schoolhouses were filled with children studying their spelling and ciphering books, completely unaware that a deadly atmospheric guillotine was falling from the north.

19
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:48.600
[George] Homestead families had spent the mild morning washing laundry, airing out sawed houses, and sending their children to school without heavy winter overcoats, rejoicing in what felt like the early arrival of spring.

20
00:02:48.600 --> 00:03:03.400
[George] High in the subarctic reaches of the Canadian Northwest Territories, an immense, compressed reservoir of supercooled Siberian polar air had accumulated, pushed southward by a violent atmospheric pressure gradient exceeding 40 millibars.

21
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:12.120
[George] Around noon, teachers and students looking out schoolhouse windows noticed an ominous, terrifying transformation along the northern horizon.

22
00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:22.760
[George] The clear blue sky was suddenly swallowed by a towering, pitch-black cloud of ice fog and blown snow that rolled across the prairie like a roaring tidal wave of black ink.

23
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:33.320
[George] Accompanied by a low, deafening hum that sounded like a squadron of freight trains thundering across a wooden trestle, the Arctic cold front struck with cataclysmic speed.

24
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:46.880
[George] In less than three minutes, the temperature dropped 40 degrees, plunging from nearly forty above zero to twenty below zero, eventually bottoming out at 40 degrees below zero with sustained wind chills nearing minus 80.

25
00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:57.520
[George] Hurricane force winds of 60 to 70 miles per hour swept across the plains, picking up fine, crystalline snow and pulverizing it into microscopic ice needles.

26
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:04.560
[George] The blizzard was so dense and white that a person holding their hand in front of their face could not see their own fingertips.

27
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.880
[George] Breathing became agonizing as ice crystals choked the nostrils and froze the eyelashes shut in seconds.

28
00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.800
[George] Cows in pastures suffocated, standing upright as solid masks of ice formed over their muzzles.

29
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:27.840
[George] The Arctic gale descended with shocking speed, plunging temperatures 40 degrees in 20 minutes as a wall of pulverized ice particles turned the sun into a ghostly white haze.

30
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:42.480
[George] Across hundreds of miles of Dakota territory, Nebraska and Minnesota, the sudden disaster placed life or death decisions squarely upon the shoulders of young schoolteachers, many of them teenage girls barely older than the students they taught.

31
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.640
[George] School was scheduled to dismiss around three or four in the afternoon.

32
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.680
[George] Teachers had to make an agonizing calculation.

33
00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:58.640
[George] Should they keep the children inside the frail, uninsulated wooden schoolhouses, where fuel for the cast-iron potbellied stoves was rapidly running out?

34
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:05.040
[George] Or should they attempt to lead them through the blinding whiteout to the nearest homestead only a few hundred yards away?

35
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.920
[George] In the flat, roadless expanse of the prairie, a distance of even fifty paces in a blizzard was a death trap.

36
00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:19.360
[George] Men frequently became disoriented just walking from their houses to their barns and froze to death within feet of their own kitchen doors.

37
00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:29.840
[George] Some teachers panicked and dismissed their classes, sending small children out into the howling vortex where they wandered aimlessly in circles until freezing to death in snowdrifts.

38
00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:42.120
[George] But in dozens of schoolhouses, young teachers performed acts of unimaginable heroism, burning wooden desks, chairs and schoolbooks in the stove to keep their students alive through the freezing night.

39
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:52.840
[George] Mii Hunt tied a spool of twine to the schoolhouse door latch and wrapped the cord around her wrists, hurting her students in a tight human chain through the blinding vortex toward her home.

40
00:05:52.840 --> 00:06:01.320
[George] Among the most celebrated heroes of the catastrophe was Minnie Freeman, a 19-year-old schoolteacher in Myra Valley, Nebraska.

41
00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:14.840
[George] When the Galeforce winds struck her schoolhouse, the howling draft ripped the wooden door from its hinges, and the tarpaper roof was torn away by the wind, exposing her seventeen young students to the howling Sub Zero blizzard.

42
00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:21.720
[George] Realizing that the building was about to collapse and that they would freeze within an hour, Minnie made a daring decision.

43
00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:32.040
[George] She gathered her seventeen frightened pupils, tied them together in a single file line using heavy hemp twine, and wrapped them in whatever coats and blankets were available.

44
00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:42.600
[George] Stepping out into the blinding whiteout, Minnie placed herself at the head of the human chain, keeping her students close together and shouting words of encouragement above the roar of the wind.

45
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:58.360
[George] Battling suffocating snowdrifts that reached her chest, Minnie navigated entirely by instinct and memory, walking nearly half a mile across the howling prairie until she safely delivered every single one of her seventeen students to the warm home of a local homesteader.

46
00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:04.760
[George] Minnie Freeman became an international sensation, praised across the nation as the heroine of the blizzard.

47
00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:16.880
[George] Minnie Freeman used a length of braided clothesline to tether thirteen young children together, leading them through waste-deep drifts for half a mile to safety while the schoolhouse roof tore away behind them.

48
00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.200
[George] Tragically, other school marches ended in unspeakable heartbreak.

49
00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:29.520
[George] In Beadle County, Dakota Territory, young teacher Mae Hunt attempted to lead her students to a farmhouse just 200 yards away.

50
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.600
[George] In the blinding whiteout, the children lost their footing in deep snow drifts and became separated.

51
00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:41.520
[George] Search parties the next morning found several small children frozen to death within sight of safety.

52
00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.760
[George] In another horrific tragedy near Huron, Dakota Territory.

53
00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:59.520
[George] Nineteen-year-old teacher Lois Collins kept her pupils inside the schoolhouse until nightfall, but when the wooden building began to break apart under 70 mile per hour gusts, she attempted to lead them to her father's nearby homestead.

54
00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:05.840
[George] Blinded by the swirling ice needles, the group drifted past the farm and out into the open prairie.

55
00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:13.600
[George] Lois sheltered her small charges beneath her own body in a snowbank, freezing to death while desperately trying to keep them warm.

56
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:24.160
[George] Across the territory, frantic parents tied ropes to their doorframes and ventured into the blinding maelstrom, searching in vain for children who had vanished into the white abyss.

57
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:36.800
[George] On the morning of Friday, January 13, the wind finally subsided, the clouds parted, and a brilliant, mocking sun rose over a frozen, sparkling landscape of pristine white snow.

58
00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.440
[George] The sight that greeted the surviving homesteaders was one of catastrophic horror.

59
00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:50.000
[George] Across the northern plains, over 230 people had frozen to death, the vast majority of them young schoolchildren and teachers.

60
00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.800
[George] Search parties on horseback and snowshoes combed the prairie, uncovering small frozen mounds in the snowdrifts.

61
00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:07.640
[George] In many cases, older brothers were found frozen over the bodies of their younger sisters, having wrapped their own coats around their siblings in a desperate, futile effort to save them.

62
00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:12.920
[George] Thousands of cattle and horses lay frozen solid in ravines and against fence lines.

63
00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:26.840
[George] The trauma of the disaster scarred entire frontier communities for generations, leaving behind mass graves in small prairie cemeteries where multiple children from a single family were buried side by side in small pine coffins.

64
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:35.800
[George] The horrific human toll of the schoolchildren's blizzard of 1888 ignited nationwide outrage and transformed the science of meteorology.

65
00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:49.160
[George] At the time, weather forecasting in the United States was conducted by the Army Signal Corps, whose telegraphic reports were slow, centralized in Washington, and often arrived hours after storms had already struck.

66
00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:55.320
[George] The disaster exposed the criminal inadequacy of federal weather prediction for the Western territories.

67
00:09:55.320 --> 00:10:11.240
[George] In response to public fury, Congress reorganizational meteorological services, taking weather forecasting away from the military and creating the civilian United States Weather Bureau in 1890, the precursor to today's National Weather Service.

68
00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:22.680
[George] Telegraph stations were linked directly across the Great Plains to issue immediate cold wave warnings, ensuring that no sudden Arctic Front could ever strike an unsuspecting population again.

69
00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:39.640
[George] Today, monuments across North Dakota, South Dakota, and Nebraska honor the courageous teachers and innocent children of 1888, whose sacrifice helped civilize the wild frontier and established the modern systems of weather protection that safeguard millions today.

70
00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:54.000
[Rebecca] The schoolchildren's Blizzard of 1888 remains one of the most poignant and heartbreaking chapters in American history, reminding us of the immense sacrifices made by pioneer families who settled the northern plains.

71
00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:00.240
[Rebecca] It proves that behind every prairie settlement lies a story of quiet heroism and enduring love.

72
00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.280
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

73
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:04.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

74
00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:11.600
[Rebecca] Join us next Tuesday as we ride into the lawless gold camp of Deadwood and witness Wild Bill Hickock's final hand.

75
00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.640
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

